C = [
("h1", "8. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "Claim: the volume’s four tests answer the objections best made against them, and where an objector is right the volume says so. Modern writers who support parts of the argument are named beside those who press against it, each for the position the author has read in a free text. Where no named writer has been read, the objection is stated in the author’s own words and credited to no one."),
("sub", "8.1 Objection: no test works in the moment (Mariottini)"),
("p", "Claude Mariottini, an Old Testament scholar who taught at Northern Baptist Seminary, wrote in a post of 9 December 2008 that a hearer has no firm criteria for telling a true prophet from a false one at the moment the prophet speaks, and that fulfilment, the test of Deuteronomy 18:22, confirms the matter only afterwards. This is the most serious objection to a volume that offers tests, and the first half of it is right."),
("p", "Reply. Chapter 4 says the same thing: the test of fulfilment acts after the event, and Micaiah could not be known by it until Ahab was dead (1 Kings 22:28, 34–38). The volume does not claim a mechanical test that a hearer can run in a moment. It claims that the prophets give three other tests, and that two of them are available before the event. The hearer can ask whether a word leads him from the LORD. Moses gave Israel that question for exactly the case in which the sign has come true: “the LORD your God proveth you, to know whether ye love the LORD your God” (Deuteronomy 13:3). And he can ask whether the word comes from the LORD or from the prophet’s own heart, with the conduct of the prophet as evidence (Jeremiah 23:14, 16). The test of source is harder for the hearer than for the prophet. Jeremiah answered Hananiah when “the word of the LORD came unto Jeremiah” (Jeremiah 28:12). The hearer does not have that. He has the commandments, the fruit of the prophet’s life and the effect of the word on the people (Jeremiah 23:22). It is not certainty. The text does not promise certainty, and the volume does not either."),
("sub", "8.2 Objection: Deuteronomy 18 is of no use to a king (Diamond)"),
("p", "James A. Diamond, of the University of Waterloo, wrote in an article of 20 August 2020 on TheTorah.com that the test of Deuteronomy 18 is of no use to a king who must decide before the outcome is known. He adds, of the lying spirit in 1 Kings 22, that “the lying spirit that enticed him to this folly was himself.” Diamond means that Ahab was already inclined to hear what he wished."),
("p", "Reply. On the first point the volume agrees, and chapter 4 says it in the same terms. A test that acts after the event cannot decide a war council. That is why the volume places the tests of source and loyalty in front of it, and why Jehoshaphat asks the right question before the event: “Is there not here a prophet of the LORD besides, that we might enquire of him?” (1 Kings 22:7). On the second point the volume partly agrees. Ahab did hear what he wished, and he had said that he hated the prophet who spoke evil of him (1 Kings 22:8). But the text goes further than Diamond’s sentence. Micaiah says, “the LORD hath put a lying spirit in the mouth of all these thy prophets” (1 Kings 22:23). The act is given to the LORD, as judgment on a king who had shut his ears. Diamond’s observation about Ahab’s own inclination fortifies the reading that the judgment fell on a man who had already chosen. It does not remove the LORD from the verse."),
("sub", "8.3 Objection: the criteria leave doubt (Fortier)"),
("p", "Janick Fortier, in a 2022 master’s thesis at Trinity Western University on discerning true and false prophets in Jeremiah, treats the criteria of person and message that the book supplies. He concludes that they “do not eliminate all doubts” but are “useful enough to inspire confidence.” The thesis treats Hananiah in Jeremiah 28 within that argument."),
("p", "Reply. The volume has no quarrel with Fortier’s conclusion and counts it as support. The tests do not remove every doubt. They give a hearer grounds for confidence, and they catch the errors that matter most: a word from a man’s own heart, a word that leads from the LORD, a word that the event refutes. The volume adds that the doubt is not equal on all sides. A false word that leads Israel from the LORD is refused whether or not the hearer is certain how the prophet came by it (Deuteronomy 13:3, 4)."),
("sub", "8.4 Objection: prophets are known by their conduct (Wessels)"),
("p", "Wilhelm J. Wessels, writing in the Journal for Semitics (2017), reads the rival prophets of Jeremiah 23:9–15 as known to be false by their conduct and because the LORD did not send them (Jeremiah 23:21). He supports chapter 2 as far as it goes. Conduct is real evidence. The volume has said that it is evidence and not the test, and Wessels’s own second ground, that the LORD did not send them, is the test."),
("sub", "8.5 Objection: Amos 3:7 means the prophets knew everything"),
("p", "No named writer is cited for this objection, and the author states it in his own words. It says that if the LORD “revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets” (Amos 3:7), then the prophets must have been told every purpose of God, including the one Paul calls a mystery. Reply. The verse says the LORD “will do” nothing without revealing it. It speaks of what he is about to do in the history of his dealings with Israel and the nations. First Peter 1:11 shows that the prophets themselves searched “what, or what manner of time” the Spirit signified, which shows they were not told everything. And Prophecy & Mystery Volume 2 argues that the Mystery was not foretold by the prophets. Amos 3:7 is a principle about announced events, and it is not a claim about the whole counsel of God."),
("sub", "8.6 Objection: Jonah proves that prophets can be wrong"),
("p", "This is stated in the author’s own words and credited to no one. It says that Jonah announced the overthrow of Nineveh in forty days, that Nineveh was not overthrown, and that a prophet’s word can therefore fail. Reply. It can fail only if the test of Deuteronomy 18:22 is read alone. Chapter 5 shows that the LORD stated the rule of Jeremiah 18:7–10, that the Ninevites read the word as conditional (Jonah 3:9), that the LORD spared the city on its turning (Jonah 3:10), and that Jonah himself knew it would be so (Jonah 4:2). The word prospered “in the thing whereto I sent it” (Isaiah 55:11). It did not fail."),
("sub", "8.7 Objection: private interpretation is about the reader"),
("p", "Many commentaries, in the author’s experience of them, read 2 Peter 1:20 as a rule for the reader: no one is to interpret Scripture on his own. No single writer is named here, because no text has been read in full that holds the view. Reply. The reading is possible, and the volume does not rule out that Peter’s words bear on how Scripture is read. But the word “For” at the start of verse 21 gives the ground of verse 20, and the ground is the origin of prophecy: “the prophecy came not in old time by the will of man.” A sentence that explains why prophecy is not of private interpretation by saying where prophecy came from is, on its face, about origin. The volume prefers the reading the grammar gives and holds its judgment of the other reading lightly."),
("sub", "8.8 Objection: Numbers 23:19 contradicts Jeremiah 18 and Jonah 3"),
("p", "Again no writer is named. The objection says that if God “is not a man, that he should lie; neither the son of man, that he should repent” (Numbers 23:19), then Scripture cannot say that he repented of the evil he said he would do (Jonah 3:10). Reply. Chapter 6 shows that the same chapter of 1 Samuel uses the verb both ways, and that the LORD marks a word he will not turn back by its wording. The objection treats the two kinds of word as one. The volume holds that they are two, and that the prophets keep them apart."),
("ctbl", "The objections and the replies in one table.", [
    ("Objection", "Held by", "Reply in one line"),
    ("No test works in the moment", "Mariottini", "Agreed for fulfilment; source and loyalty are available before the event (Deuteronomy 13:3; Jeremiah 23:16)"),
    ("Deuteronomy 18 is useless to a king", "Diamond", "Agreed; hence the tests that come first; the LORD still gives the act in 1 Kings 22:23"),
    ("The criteria leave doubt", "Fortier", "Agreed; the tests give confidence, not certainty"),
    ("Conduct is the mark", "Wessels (partly)", "Conduct is evidence; sending is the test (Jeremiah 23:21)"),
    ("Amos 3:7 means the prophets knew all", "none named", "It speaks of announced events; 1 Peter 1:11 shows they searched"),
    ("Jonah proves prophets can be wrong", "none named", "Deuteronomy 18:22 read with Jeremiah 18:7–10; Jonah 4:2"),
    ("Private interpretation is about the reader", "none named", "The “For” of 2 Peter 1:21 grounds it in origin"),
    ("Numbers 23:19 contradicts Jonah 3:10", "none named", "Two kinds of word, marked by their wording (1 Samuel 15)"),
  ], [0.34, 0.2, 0.46]),
("sub", "8.9 What the volume takes from the replies"),
("bul", [
    "The volume concedes that fulfilment acts only after the event and that no test gives certainty.",
    "The tests of source and loyalty are available before the event, and the volume rests its weight on them.",
    "Modern writers fortify the claims about source, conduct and confidence and press on the limits of fulfilment; the volume names each for what the author has read.",
    "Where no writer has been read, the objection is stated in the author’s words and answered from the text.",
  ]),
]
